  <span class="hero-ornament"></span>
  <h1 class="research-title">History Now</h1>
  <p class="ref-lede">What is being found and written about the past this week, the round anniversaries of the next thirty days, and what is new at the Institute.</p>
</header>

<section class="now-sec now-sec-news" id="news" aria-labelledby="now-news-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-news-t">In the news</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Headlines from other publications, newest first, each with the Institute&rsquo;s page on its subject. The headlines, the articles and the thumbnails their feeds offer belong to their publishers: the Institute has not written or checked them. A headline without a publisher&rsquo;s thumbnail shows the picture of the Institute&rsquo;s page instead. The list is refreshed twice a day.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="news" data-max="10"><li class="now-wait">The headlines need JavaScript.</li></ol>
  <p class="now-note now-src">From the news pages of <a href="https://archaeology.org/news/" rel="noopener">Archaeology Magazine</a>, <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/" rel="noopener">Smithsonian Magazine</a>, <a href="https://phys.org/science-news/archaeology/" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a>, <a href="https://archaeologymag.com/" rel="noopener">Archaeology News</a>, <a href="https://www.medievalists.net/" rel="noopener">Medievalists.net</a>, <a href="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/" rel="noopener">The History Blog</a>. A headline is listed only when it names a page of the Institute.</p>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="anniversaries" aria-labelledby="now-ann-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-ann-t">Coming anniversaries</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Round anniversaries in the next thirty days: centuries, half-centuries, quarter-centuries of the last three hundred years and the decades of the last hundred. Every day has its own page in <a href="/today-in-history/">Today in History</a>.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="ann" data-max="40"><li class="now-wait">The anniversaries need JavaScript.</li></ol>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="new" aria-labelledby="now-new-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-new-t">New at the Institute</h2>
  <ol class="wn-list">
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-14">14 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">How the Institute works, and a public list of corrections</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page sets out where each kind of page comes from, how drafts are written and checked against their sources, and the rules the texts follow. A second lists every correction of fact made so far, 774 of them, with what was wrong and what replaced it.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/method">How the Institute works</a> <a href="/corrections">Corrections</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">131 entries told in more detail</h3><p class="wn-s">The entries readers reach most often from search now go further than a paragraph: background, the course of events and what followed for wars and treaties, early life, career and legacy for people, setting and history for places, each written from its Wikipedia article and linked like the rest. Among them the Sonderbund War, the Ottonian dynasty, Dara Shikoh, Hoelun, the Gortyn Code and the Bodleian Library.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/">The encyclopedia</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Pictures for 230 more entries, and dates for 41 people</h3><p class="wn-s">Margaret Thatcher, David Ben-Gurion, the Beatles, the Battle of Stalingrad, the Six-Day War, Shostakovich, national anthems in their first prints and some 220 more entries now have a picture, each chosen by eye. Forty-one people who had no dates are placed by the decade or century their own entries give, and so appear on the century and people pages.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/">The encyclopedia</a> <a href="/people/">People</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twenty more great lives, and pictures in the first twenty</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty more lives told in full: Hammurabi, Ramesses II, Aristotle, Hannibal, Qin Shi Huang, Charlemagne, Harun al-Rashid, Rumi, Kublai Khan, Pachacuti, Joan of Arc, Martin Luther, Suleiman the Magnificent, Tokugawa Ieyasu, Isaac Newton, Catherine the Great, George Washington, Toussaint Louverture, Liliʻuokalani and Haile Selassie. The sections of all forty lives now carry pictures.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=life">Great lives</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Great lives: twenty people told in full</h3><p class="wn-s">A new kind of profile: twenty lives from Cyrus the Great, Confucius and Ashoka to Elizabeth I, Simón Bolívar and Nelson Mandela, each with its early life, rise, deeds, opponents and critics, last years and legacy, key events and references.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=life">Great lives</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Rulers on the profiles, and who was born near any place</h3><p class="wn-s">The profiles of 174 states and empires now list their rulers and leaders among the people of the encyclopedia, each a person whose own entry names them as that state’s ruler. On the atlas, “What was here?” also lists the people born within 250 km of the place you choose.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/atlas">The atlas</a> <a href="/profiles">Profiles</a></p></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="now-note"><a href="/new">Everything that is new, and every page added in the last thirty days &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
